/E (The relational model stores data in tables made of rows and columns. A primary key identifies each row, and a foreign key links a row to a row in another table [1]. Data is read and changed using SQL \(Structured Query Language\). The model is simple, and it has strong support for rules that keep data correct. )
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
409 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 194 0 R
/E (An ER model describes a system using entities \(things we store data about\), attributes \(their properties\) and relationships \(how entities are connected\) [2]. Relationships have a cardinality such as one-to-many \(1:N\) or many-to-many \(M:N\). When an ER model is converted into tables, three common rules are used: a 1:N relationship puts a foreign key on the "many" side; an M:N relationship becomes a separate table that holds both keys; and a 1:1 relationship uses a foreign key with a UNIQUE constraint. )
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
