438 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 195 0 R
/E (A trigger is a small program stored in the database that runs automatically when a row is inserted, updated or deleted. It can run BEFORE the change, to check or adjust it, or AFTER the change, to update other tables. A trigger can stop a change by raising an error with the SIGNAL statement. A CHECK constraint can only look at the row being saved, so triggers are used for rules that need to look at other rows or tables, such as overlapping trips [5]. )
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [20]
>>
endobj
439 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 195 0 R
/E (A transaction groups several steps so that they all succeed or all fail \(COMMIT or ROLLBACK\). The InnoDB storage engine of MySQL supports transactions and keeps data consistent when many users work at the same time [3]. A UNIQUE index is checked by the database itself, so even two clerks booking the same seat at the same moment cannot both )
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [21]
>>
endobj
440 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 195 0 R
/E (An index is a separate structure that lets the database find rows quickly without reading the whole table. MySQL uses B-tree indexes for most columns. Indexes make searches faster but add a small cost to every insert and update, so they are created only on columns that are searched often, such as the source and destination city of a route or the departure time of a trip. )
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [22]
>>
endobj
